<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Mosquito Shore</h1><p class="st-meta">1749 CE – 1787 CE · capital Black River</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">British protectorate on the Caribbean coast of Honduras and Nicaragua, built on an alliance with the Miskito kings and given up to Spain in 1787.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>English traders and buccaneers had been allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miskito">Miskito people</a> since the 17th century, and Jamaican governors crowned Miskito kings. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-jenkins-ear">War of Jenkins' Ear</a> <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> sent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-hodgson-mosquito-shore">Robert Hodgson</a> to the coast, and in 1749 a superintendency was formally established, with its headquarters at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-river-honduras">Black River</a> on the Honduran coast.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A British superintendent, appointed by the governor of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/jamaica">Jamaica</a>, oversaw a few hundred settlers and their enslaved workers, who cut mahogany, traded turtle shell, sarsaparilla and hides and smuggled goods into Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miskito">Miskito</a> kings and chiefs remained powerful allies, raiding Spanish settlements and capturing other indigenous people for sale. Settlers sat in a council but quarrelled with the superintendents.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/convention-of-london-1786">Convention of London</a> of 1786 <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> agreed to evacuate the Mosquito Shore in exchange for an enlarged logging area in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/belize">Belize</a>. In 1787 about 2,000 settlers and enslaved people moved to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/belize-settlement">Belize Settlement</a>. British influence on the coast revived in the 19th century and led to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mosquito-reserve">Mosquito Reserve</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/mosquito-shore.jpg" alt="Mosquito Shore" width="320" height="250"><figcaption>Image: Rowanwindwhistler; derivative work by Rowanwindwhistler · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapa_Miskito-es.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1749 CE – 1787 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Black River</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Robert Hodgson, James Lawrie</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Anglicanism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English and Miskito</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/honduras">Honduras</a>, <a href="/countries/nicaragua">Nicaragua</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>British Mosquito Shore, Black River settlement</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1768&amp;focus=mosquito-shore" aria-label="Mosquito Shore on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2355 1676 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Mosquito Shore lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2675 1932L4997 651"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4997 651h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2675 1932h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2675 1932h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1768&amp;focus=mosquito-shore">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

